{
  "description": "Test cases for the ID-number hint, which is computed from the ID type's regex because the API returns no example. Taken once from the 2026-09-28 sandbox responses to cover the syntax the server uses, plus synthetic cases outside the subset; they test the algorithm, so they change only when it does. Each platform asserts that its hint equals `hint`, that every non-null hint fully matches its own regex, and that every regex compiles on that platform's engine.",
  "algorithm": [
    "Strip a leading ^ and a trailing $.",
    "Take the first alternative, at the top level and inside every group; (?: … ) is a group too.",
    "A character class becomes the first of A, 0 and a that it accepts, otherwise its first member; \\d becomes 0 and \\w becomes A; an escaped or plain literal stays as it is.",
    "Repeat each part: {m} uses m, {m,n} uses n, {m,} uses m (at least one), * and ? use none, + uses one. A repeat over 64 is outside the subset, so a server regex cannot make the example allocate without limit.",
    "Anything else — ., a negated class, a lookaround, a backreference, \\S and other escaped letters, a lazy or possessive quantifier — is outside the subset: `hint` is null."
  ],
  "display": {
    "hint": "e.g. {hint}",
    "fallback": "Enter your {label}",
    "untilTypeChosen": "Choose an ID type first"
  },
  "check": "The number is trimmed and must match the whole regex before Continue enables. A blank regex, or one that fails to compile on the device, shows the fallback and checks nothing, leaving the server to judge the number.",
